    "text": "96\n\n How to make sure you get a good deal when buying or selling and accounting practice\n\n The Stages of a Deal: A Step-by-Step Guide\nFrom Sara Sharp: Navigating the Process of Buying an Accounting Firm\n\nHi Everyone,\nI\u2019m Sara Sharp, and I help people buy and sell accounting firms.\u00a0\nBuying a firm can seem like a daunting process, but if you break it down into clear steps, it\u2019s much easier to manage. Here\u2019s a simple guide to help you understand each stage of the deal-making process.\n1. Determining Your Deal Box\nBefore you start looking for a firm to buy, you need to define your \u201cdeal box.\u201d This means figuring out what kind of firm you\u2019re looking for. Consider the size, location, client base, and services offered. Knowing exactly what you want will help you focus your search.\nLocation\n\nAsking Price\n\nTypes of Clients\n\nServices Offered\n\nFirm Size by Revenue or Employee Count\n\nSellers Discretionary Earnings aka Cashflow\n\n2. Searching Online Marketplaces\nOnce you know what you\u2019re looking for, start searching online marketplaces. There are many websites that specialize in listing accounting practices for sale. You can filter the search results to match your deal box criteria, ie List Price, Revenue, Cashflow, Location, etc.\nExamples:\nBizBuySell.com\n\nAccountingpracticeexchange.com\n\n3. Submitting an NDA, Personal Financial Statement, and Resume\nWhen you find a firm that fits, the next step is to submit a Non-Disclosure Agreement (NDA). The NDA ensures that all information shared between you and the seller remains confidential. Pair the NDA with a Personal Financial Statement (PFS). The Personal Financial Statement shows the seller that you have the financial ability to buy the firm. It may include proof of funds or an SBA Pre-Qualification letter. Attach your resume. This outlines your experience and qualifications to show the seller you're prepared to run the firm.\nSBA Personal Financial Statement\n4. Seller Meeting\nIf the seller is interested in moving forward, you\u2019ll have a meeting to discuss the firm in more detail. First impressions are EVERYTHING. So take great care with this call.\u00a0\nThis is your chance to ask questions, learn more about the business, and see if it\u2019s a good fit for you. But most importantly, remember this firm is their baby. Don\u2019t call their baby ugly the first time you meet. It\u2019s an opportunity to build rapport with the seller, which will be essential as you move forward.\n5. Submitting an LOI (Letter of Intent)\nAfter the seller meeting, if you decide you want to buy the firm, you\u2019ll submit a Letter of Intent (LOI). The LOI outlines the basic terms of the deal, such as the purchase price, payment terms, and any conditions that need to be met. It\u2019s not legally binding, but it shows the seller that you\u2019re serious about moving forward.\nBecome an LOI Expert here\n6. Due Diligence\nOnce the LOI is signed, the due diligence process begins. This is where you carefully review all aspects of the business. This includes financial statements, client contracts, and operations. Due diligence helps you confirm that the business is as it was represented and identify any potential risks.\nSample Due Diligence Document Request List:\nLast 3 years Income Statements\n\nLast 3 years Tax Returns\n\nCurrent Balance Sheet\n\nAR Aging Summary\n\nBank Statements\n\nGeneral Ledger\n\nEmployee List\n\nVendor List\n\nClient List\n\nContracts\n\nConsider hiring a CPA who specializes in due diligence to perform a Quality of Earnings assessment.\u00a0\n7. Securing Funding\nWhile in due diligence, you\u2019ll want to start the process to secure funding for the deal. This could involve getting a loan from a bank, finding investors, or using your own savings. It\u2019s important to have your financing in place before you move on to the final steps.\nIf you plan to apply for an SBA 7(a) loan, make sure you\u2019re working with an SBA Preferred Lender. That can save you months in processing time!\n8. Closing the Deal\nAfter securing funding, you\u2019re ready to close the deal. This is where all the final paperwork is signed, and the business officially changes hands. Closing the deal usually involves working with attorneys to ensure everything is done correctly.\n9. Transitioning Ownership\nFinally, after the deal closes, you\u2019ll need to transition ownership. This includes training from the previous owner. Introducing yourself to clients and employees. Making necessary changes to the business operations. A smooth transition is key to maintaining the firm\u2019s success and keeping clients and staff happy.\nBuying an accounting firm isn't easy. But follow these stages, and you can navigate the process with confidence. Remember, each step is important. Take your time to get things right. It will pay off in the long run.\nIf you ever have any questions or need guidance, I\u2019m here to help you through each stage of the deal.\nI hope this guide helps you feel more prepared for your next big move.\nBest,\nSara Sharp\nDeal Attorney, Partner\nSK&S Law Group\n--\nFor more helpful lessons from Deal academy, check out our Dealmakers Youtube Channel where you'll find Sara's Weekly Wine Down where she talks about what she's seen in the industry this week and interviews with M&A Experts and Dealmakers.\nYou can check out our latest Dealmakers podcast episodes by clicking the images below.\n\n Copyright \u00a9 2024\u00a0 Someday Consultants, All rights reserved. \n\n Our e-mail address is:\ninfo@dealacademy.org\n\n Want to change how you receive these emails?\n You can unsubscribe from this list.",
